Hint files are written in place instead of to a temporary file renamed into
place. The rename is what makes "a hint exists" mean "the hint is complete":
a hint that fails its CRC is rebuilt by a scan that skips the tail handling,
since a file with a hint was sealed. Recovery cuts a hint-less file's tail
before it writes the hint (#476), but the hint file is created before the
scan that finds the tail, so in place it exists, holding only its header,
while the file is still uncut. A crash then sends the next open to that
scan over an untrimmed tail, and DB::open refuses the database. (The design
first expected this to survive.) It takes a crash inside the few
milliseconds of an open's scan or hint write. Unfocused runs caught it twice
in about 45 minutes before #476 and in 6 minutes after it (seed 1, a
SIGKILL: a 16-byte hint over an uncut file); focusing on power cuts without
the latency and stalls that slow opens down caught it less, so it runs
unfocused and is not held to a budget.
Expected: caught (rare).
Guarded-by: "DB recovery: a crash while open writes a hint leaves no hint behind it\, and the next open recovers"

